#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# Gem packaging check.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. `kiosk-server.gemspec` said
# `spec.files = Dir.glob("lib/**/*") + %w[README.md LICENSE.txt CHANGELOG.md]`.
# It omitted `app/`, so the three ERB templates under
# `app/views/kiosk/server/{device_verify,assistants}/` were not in the built gem
# — while `device_verify_controller.rb` and `assistants_controller.rb`
# `append_view_path` at exactly that directory. Every consumer installing from
# RubyGems got `ActionView::MissingTemplate` on BOTH HTML pages of the
# account-binding ceremony: the entire human-facing half of the flow, gone.
#
# It shipped that way, and every suite in this repo stayed green, because THIS
# CLASS OF DEFECT IS INVISIBLE BY CONSTRUCTION HERE. Every consumer in the
# monorepo — the eight demos, the e2e fixture, the sibling gems — uses `path:`,
# which serves files straight from the working tree. The working tree always has
# app/views. No test in this repo can distinguish "the file is there" from "the
# file is packaged", so no test can notice, and none did. The only observer is
# someone who installs the gem, and there is not one of those inside CI.
#
# So this is a CHECK, not a convention. It BUILDS each gem the way RubyGems
# builds it, reads the file list back out of the resulting `.gem`, and compares
# it against what the repo contains and what the gem's own Ruby reaches for.
# "Someone must remember that spec.files is a hand-maintained list" becomes
# "CI notices when it stopped being complete".
#
# THE RULES IT ENFORCES. The first two approach one invariant from opposite
# ends, and the kiosk-server defect was visible from both; the third asks a
# different question of the same package:
#
#   A. COMPLETENESS — from the FILES.  Every git-tracked file under a gem
#      directory must be either in the built package, or matched by a pattern in
#      the NOT_PACKAGED manifest below, whose value is the REASON that kind of
#      file has no business in a gem. A file in neither fails. So does a
#      manifest pattern that matches nothing anywhere (a rule guarding nothing
#      is a rule nobody will re-read). This is what makes a NEW unpackaged file
#      impossible to add silently — the failure mode that produced the defect,
#      where `app/` was created long after `spec.files` was written and nothing
#      connected the two.
#
#   B. REACHABILITY — from the CODE.  Every path literal that packaged Ruby
#      resolves against `__dir__` must land on something that is also packaged.
#      That is the assertion that would have failed the moment
#      `append_view_path File.expand_path("../../../app/views", __dir__)` was
#      written, without anyone having to think about packaging at all.
#
#   C. THE INSTALL POINTER — from the PROSE (K-1343). A packaged file that
#      TEACHES an install of a Kiosk gem must, in that same file, point at the
#      one place publication status is stated. Same reader, same reason as A
#      and B: someone holding the built gem has this file and nothing else, so
#      an unqualified `bundle add kiosk-all` in it is an instruction that
#      answers 404 with no way to find out why. The monorepo README says of its
#      own Install section that it is «where publication status is stated
#      canonically … and every install snippet elsewhere points here instead of
#      restating it» — this rule is what makes that sentence true rather than
#      hopeful. (It used to say «the one place in either published repository
#      that states publication status», which was false the day it was written:
#      the onboarding page states it too, and now carries a DERIVED copy that
#      `bin/check-onboarding-derivation` holds to this README — K-1381.)
#      It is a THIRD invariant behind this exit code
#      because its SUBJECT is the same one: what a RubyGems reader receives.
#
# WHY BOTH. A alone passes a gem that packages every file it owns and then
# reaches for a file it does not own (kiosk-redteam did exactly this until its
# solver reach moved behind Kiosk::Pow::Equihash.solver_path on a declared
# dependency — see OUT_OF_GEM). B alone passes a gem whose runtime paths all
# resolve but whose README, generator templates or data files were dropped.
# Neither subsumes the other, and the defect that motivated this was in the
# intersection. C is orthogonal to both: the file is packaged and every path in
# it resolves, and it still tells the reader to run a command that cannot work.
#
# WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not proof the gem WORKS when installed — nothing here
# boots Rails against an installed gem or renders a template. It asserts the
# bytes are in the package. That is the whole distance between the tree and
# RubyGems, and it is the distance the defect lived in; a full install-and-boot
# gate is a different, much heavier check, and this one running in 4 s on every
# push is worth more than that one running never.
#
# THE FAIL-OPEN GUARD. Rule B reads source with regexes, and a regex that does
# not recognise a shape reports success — the exact failure mode this file
# exists to prevent. So an occurrence of `__dir__` or `__FILE__` in packaged
# Ruby that does NOT match one of the resolvable shapes is a HARD ERROR, not a
# skip: the checker says out loud that it cannot see, instead of passing.
#
# For the same reason it reads every packaged file that CAN contain Ruby, not
# just `*.rb`: a `.erb` view or a `.rake` task resolving a path against __dir__
# is the same defect wearing a different extension, and "the regex found nothing
# because it never opened the file" is precisely the shape of failure this
# guard exists to make impossible. `.tt` is deliberately NOT read — those are
# generator templates whose OUTPUT lands in the host application, so a __dir__
# in one resolves against the host's db/migrate, not against this package.
#
# EVERY MANIFEST IS CHECKED FOR STALENESS, and for the same reason: an entry
# that no longer guards anything is an entry nobody will re-read, and it is a
# standing lie about the state of the tree. A NOT_PACKAGED pattern matching no
# file, an OUT_OF_GEM literal no packaged file reaches for any more, and an
# INSTALL_MENTION_ONLY entry whose file no longer mentions an install, are all
# errors. OUT_OF_GEM especially: its entries exist to be DELETED when the
# decision they record lands, and if the code changed first nothing else would
# notice.
#
# Rule C carries one more guard the other two do not need, because its polarity
# is opposite: A and B are BANS, where «nothing matched» is the tree being
# clean, while C is a PRESENCE requirement, where «nothing matched» is the rule
# not running. So a run in which NO packaged file teaches an install FAILS.
#
# Stdlib only (rubygems + tmpdir), no bundle, no database. It shells out to
# `gem build` once per gem — deliberately the real builder rather than a
# re-implementation of `spec.files`, since trusting the glob is what went wrong.
# ~4 s for 14 gems. Same profile and same reason as bin/check-ci-tasks and
# bin/check-demo-copies.
#
# Usage:
#   bin/check-gem-packaging            # check
#   bin/check-gem-packaging --list     # print what each gem packages and reaches for
#   bin/check-gem-packaging --self-test  # red on a planted break of every rule

require "rubygems/package"
require "tmpdir"
require "shellwords"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)

# ── --self-test: one planted break per rule, in a MINIATURE workspace (K-1368)
#
# WHY THIS FILE HAS ONE, and it is not «every guard should» — that principle is
# explicitly rejected in this workspace. Two facts about THIS script are the
# argument:
#
#   * ITS NEWEST RULE IS PRESENCE-SHAPED, which is the shape that goes quietly
#     green. Rule C fails when a packaged file teaches an install WITHOUT the
#     pointer; if `INSTALL_TEACHING` stops recognising the shapes people write,
#     the rule matches nothing and this script exits 0 — the same colour as a
#     clean tree. There is an in-band vacuity guard for exactly that, and until
#     now NOTHING PROVED THE GUARD ITSELF STILL FIRES.
#   * THE PROOF WAS BEING THROWN AWAY. K-1343's rules were proven by a verify
#     pass planting breaks BY HAND — a publication-day arm and a
#     detector-switched-off arm — and neither was re-runnable afterwards. They
#     existed only as prose in a ledger row, which is the K-1227 shape: a rule
#     that reads as covered with no mechanism that goes red for it.
#
# HOW IT RUNS, AND WHY NOT ON THE REAL TREE. `check-demo-copies` copies the
# working tree and runs itself from the copy's own `bin/`; that is right for a
# guard whose subject IS the fleet. This one's subject is what `gem build`
# produces, so the fixture is a MINIATURE WORKSPACE instead: a git repository
# with two tiny gems, a monorepo README carrying the canonical install line and
# the `## Install` heading, and this script copied into its `bin/`. `ROOT` is
# derived from `__dir__`, so the copy walks the fixture and nothing here ever
# writes inside the real repository. Two gems build in well under a second,
# where fourteen take four — and the arms are aimed at RULES, which a miniature
# can carry faithfully.
#
# THE FIXTURE MUST SATISFY EVERY MANIFEST IN THIS FILE, and that is a feature:
# `NOT_PACKAGED`'s eight patterns, `OUT_OF_GEM` (empty) and
# `INSTALL_MENTION_ONLY`'s one entry are all checked for STALENESS on every run,
# so a fixture that did not carry a subject for each of them would be red before
# any arm ran. The baseline is asserted green first for exactly that reason.
#
# TWO KINDS OF ARM, declared per row:
#
#   :break    a faithful mistake in the FIXTURE — the gemspec that forgets a
#             directory (the defect this whole file exists for), a __dir__ that
#             escapes the package, a README that teaches an install and points
#             nowhere. None is a syntax error a parser would catch.
#   :vacuity  the rule's SUBJECT removed rather than violated, to prove the
#             in-band guard watching that rule's liveness still fires. Note the
#             polarity: for Rule C — a PRESENCE requirement — «nothing matched»
#             is the rule not running, and for the manifests it is an entry that
#             has stopped guarding anything.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER: it proves each rule reddens on ONE break in a
# miniature. It does not prove the rule is right, does not enumerate the breaks
# a rule could miss, and says nothing about the fourteen real gems — the bare
# run is what answers for those.
if ARGV.include?("--self-test")
  require "fileutils"
  require "open3"
  require "rbconfig"

  # ── the fixture workspace ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  #
  # Two gems, because one cannot show a manifest entry that names a gem. Every
  # file here exists to be a SUBJECT of some rule or manifest, and the comment
  # on each says which.
  ST_FIXTURE = {
    # Rule C derives its pointer from this file: the slug off the canonical
    # install line, the anchor off the heading. Both are planted against.
    "README.md" => <<~'MD',
      # Fixture monorepo

      ## Install

      Nothing here is published.

      ```ruby
      gem "kiosk-all", github: "kiosk-hq/kiosk"
      ```
    MD

    # ── kiosk-server: the gem that carries a subject for every manifest ──
    #
    # NAMED `kiosk-server` on purpose: INSTALL_MENTION_ONLY declares a path in
    # that gem, and a manifest entry naming a gem the workspace does not have is
    # a stale entry — so a fixture without it would be red before any arm ran.
    "kiosk-server/kiosk-server.gemspec" => <<~'RB',
      Gem::Specification.new do |spec|
        spec.name        = "kiosk-server"
        spec.version     = "0.0.0"
        spec.summary     = "self-test fixture"
        spec.description = "A miniature gem, built by bin/check-gem-packaging --self-test."
        spec.authors     = ["fixture"]
        spec.email       = ["fixture@example.invalid"]
        spec.homepage    = "https://kiosk.tech"
        spec.license     = "Apache-2.0"
        spec.files       = Dir.glob("lib/**/*") + Dir.glob("app/**/*") + %w[README.md]
        spec.require_paths = ["lib"]
      end
    RB
    # Rule B's subject: a packaged file resolving a path against __dir__ that
    # lands on another packaged path. This is the shape of the defect the whole
    # script exists for.
    "kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server.rb" => <<~'RB',
      module Kiosk
        module Server
          VIEWS = File.expand_path("../../app/views", __dir__)
        end
      end
    RB
    # INSTALL_MENTION_ONLY's declared subject: prose ABOUT the resolver that
    # names an install command without instructing anyone to run it.
    "kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server/request_validation.rb" => <<~'RB',
      # A missing runtime dependency is what `gem install kiosk-server` resolves;
      # run `bundle install` rather than reaching for a registry.
      module Kiosk
        module Server
          module RequestValidation
          end
        end
      end
    RB
    "kiosk-server/app/views/fixture.html.erb" => "<p>fixture view</p>\n",
    # Rule C's ordinary subject: a PACKAGED file that teaches an install and
    # therefore owes the pointer.
    "kiosk-server/README.md" => <<~'MD',
      # kiosk-server (fixture)

      ```ruby
      gem "kiosk-server"
      ```

      No Kiosk gem is on RubyGems — see https://github.com/kiosk-hq/kiosk#install.
    MD
    # NOT_PACKAGED's eight patterns, one subject each. A pattern with no subject
    # anywhere is reported stale, so these are what keep the baseline green.
    "kiosk-server/.gitignore" => "/pkg\n",
    "kiosk-server/.rspec"     => "--require spec_helper\n",
    "kiosk-server/Gemfile"    => "source \"https://rubygems.org\"\ngemspec\n",
    "kiosk-server/Gemfile.lock" => "PATH\n  remote: .\n",
    "kiosk-server/Rakefile"   => "task :default => :spec\n",
    "kiosk-server/spec/spec_helper.rb" => "# fixture spec helper\n",
    "kiosk-server/test/fixture_test.rb" => "# fixture minitest file\n",

    # ── kiosk-core: a second gem, so `listing.size` is plural and a manifest
    # entry naming a gem is distinguishable from one naming a path.
    "kiosk-core/kiosk-core.gemspec" => <<~'RB',
      Gem::Specification.new do |spec|
        spec.name        = "kiosk-core"
        spec.version     = "0.0.0"
        spec.summary     = "self-test fixture"
        spec.description = "A miniature gem, built by bin/check-gem-packaging --self-test."
        spec.authors     = ["fixture"]
        spec.email       = ["fixture@example.invalid"]
        spec.homepage    = "https://kiosk.tech"
        spec.license     = "Apache-2.0"
        spec.files       = Dir.glob("lib/**/*") + %w[README.md]
        spec.require_paths = ["lib"]
      end
    RB
    "kiosk-core/lib/kiosk/core.rb" => "module Kiosk\n  module Core\n  end\nend\n",
    "kiosk-core/README.md" => <<~'MD',
      # kiosk-core (fixture)

      Install with `bundle add kiosk-core` — see https://github.com/kiosk-hq/kiosk#install.
    MD
    "kiosk-core/.gitignore" => "/pkg\n",
  }.freeze

  # THE PLANT TABLE. `ops:` are [relative path, needle, replacement] applied to
  # the fixture; a needle that no longer occurs FAILS the arm rather than running
  # it, because a plant that changed nothing proves nothing. `expect:` must
  # appear in the planted run's output, and the run must exit 1.
  ST_PLANTS = [
    { id: "A-gemspec-forgets-a-directory", kind: :break, rule: "A. COMPLETENESS",
      what: "`spec.files` stops naming app/, exactly as kiosk-server's did — the defect this file exists for",
      ops: [["kiosk-server/kiosk-server.gemspec", ' + Dir.glob("app/**/*")', ""]],
      expect: "`app/views/fixture.html.erb` is tracked in this repo but is NOT in the built gem" },
    { id: "A-manifest-entry-guards-nothing", kind: :vacuity, rule: "A. COMPLETENESS",
      what: "the only file a NOT_PACKAGED pattern matches is deleted, leaving the pattern excusing nothing",
      ops: [[:delete, "kiosk-server/test/fixture_test.rb"]],
      expect: "NOT_PACKAGED pattern `test/**/*` matched no tracked-but-unpackaged file" },

    { id: "B-reach-outside-the-package", kind: :break, rule: "B. REACHABILITY",
      what: "packaged Ruby resolves a __dir__-relative path at a directory the gem does not ship",
      ops: [["kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server.rb", '"../../app/views"', '"../../config/views"']],
      expect: "which is NOT in the built gem" },
    { id: "B-escape-the-gem-root", kind: :break, rule: "B. REACHABILITY",
      what: "the literal climbs out of the gem entirely — it can only exist in this monorepo checkout",
      ops: [["kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server.rb", '"../../app/views"', '"../../../kiosk-core/lib"']],
      expect: "which escapes the gem root entirely" },
    { id: "B-unreadable-dir-shape", kind: :break, rule: "B. REACHABILITY",
      what: "a __dir__ in a shape RESOLVABLE does not know — the fail-open guard, which must ERROR not skip",
      ops: [["kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server.rb", 'File.expand_path("../../app/views", __dir__)',
             'File.expand_path(File.join("..", "..", "app", "views"), __dir__)']],
      expect: "It does not understand the shape used here" },

    { id: "C-packaged-file-points-nowhere", kind: :break, rule: "C. THE INSTALL POINTER",
      what: "a packaged README teaches `gem \"kiosk-server\"` and never says the gem is unpublished",
      ops: [["kiosk-server/README.md", " — see https://github.com/kiosk-hq/kiosk#install", ""]],
      expect: "teaches an install of a Kiosk gem, but does not point at" },
    { id: "C-derivation-loses-the-slug", kind: :break, rule: "C. THE INSTALL POINTER",
      what: "publication day: the canonical line loses its `github:` qualifier and the rule can no longer derive its subject",
      ops: [["README.md", 'gem "kiosk-all", github: "kiosk-hq/kiosk"', 'gem "kiosk-all"']],
      expect: "declares no canonical `gem \"kiosk-all\", github: \"...\"` line" },
    { id: "C-derivation-loses-the-heading", kind: :break, rule: "C. THE INSTALL POINTER",
      what: "the README's Install section is renamed, so the anchor the pointer names stops existing",
      ops: [["README.md", "## Install", "## Getting started"]],
      expect: "has no `## Install` heading" },
    { id: "C-detector-matches-nothing", kind: :vacuity, rule: "C. THE INSTALL POINTER",
      what: "no packaged file teaches an install any more — for a PRESENCE rule that is the rule not running",
      ops: [["kiosk-server/README.md", 'gem "kiosk-server"', "kiosk_server_setup"],
            ["kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server/request_validation.rb", "gem install kiosk-server",
             "the dependency resolution"],
            ["kiosk-core/README.md", "bundle add kiosk-core", "vendoring it"]],
      expect: "found NONE that teaches an install of a Kiosk gem" },
    { id: "C-mention-only-outlives-its-reason", kind: :vacuity, rule: "C. THE INSTALL POINTER",
      what: "the declared prose-only sentence is rewritten and the exemption is left standing",
      ops: [["kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server/request_validation.rb", "gem install kiosk-server",
             "the dependency resolution"]],
      expect: "no longer mentions installing a Kiosk gem at all" },
  ].freeze

  if ARGV.include?("--list")
    puts "check-gem-packaging --self-test --list: #{ST_PLANTS.length} arm(s) over " \
         "#{ST_PLANTS.map { |p| p[:rule] }.uniq.length} rule(s)"
    ST_PLANTS.group_by { |p| p[:rule] }.each do |rule, arms|
      puts "  #{rule}"
      arms.each { |a| puts format("    %-6s %-34s %s", a[:kind] == :vacuity ? "V" : "B", a[:id], a[:what]) }
    end
    exit 0
  end

  st_bad = []
  Dir.mktmpdir("check-gem-packaging-self-test") do |tmp|
    ST_FIXTURE.each do |rel, body|
      path = File.join(tmp, rel)
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.dirname(path))
      File.write(path, body)
    end
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.join(tmp, "bin"))
    FileUtils.copy_file(__FILE__, File.join(tmp, "bin", File.basename(__FILE__)), true)
    File.chmod(0o755, File.join(tmp, "bin", File.basename(__FILE__)))

    # Rule A reads `git ls-files`, so the fixture is a real repository. The index
    # alone answers that question, so nothing is committed — and every git call
    # excludes the machine's global config, because this must behave the same on
    # a box with a global excludesFile, a signing key or a hooksPath.
    git_cfg = ["-c", "core.excludesFile=/dev/null", "-c", "core.hooksPath=/dev/null",
               "-c", "user.name=fixture", "-c", "user.email=fixture@example.invalid"]
    _o, git_ok = Open3.capture2e("git", "-C", tmp, *git_cfg, "init", "-q")
    _o2, add_ok = Open3.capture2e("git", "-C", tmp, *git_cfg, "add", "-A")
    unless git_ok.success? && add_ok.success?
      warn "check-gem-packaging --self-test: FAIL — could not make the fixture a git repository, and " \
           "Rule A reads `git ls-files`. Nothing below would mean anything."
      exit 1
    end

    st_run = lambda do
      out, status = Open3.capture2e(RbConfig.ruby, File.join(tmp, "bin", File.basename(__FILE__)))
      [out, status.exitstatus]
    end

    base_out, base_code = st_run.call
    if base_code.zero?
      puts format("  %-6s %s", "GREEN", "baseline: the untouched fixture exits 0, so every arm below is " \
                                        "ONE planted break away from red")
    else
      st_bad << "the BASELINE fixture is not green (exit #{base_code.inspect}). No arm below means " \
                "anything until it is:\n" +
                base_out.lines.grep(/✗/).first(6).map { |l| "      #{l.rstrip[0, 180]}" }.join("\n")
    end

    if st_bad.empty?
      ST_PLANTS.each do |plant|
        bodies  = {}
        deleted = []
        aborted = false
        plant[:ops].each do |op|
          if op.first == :delete
            rel = op[1]
            unless File.file?(File.join(tmp, rel))
              st_bad << "#{plant[:id]}: the fixture no longer carries #{rel}, so this arm deletes " \
                        "nothing and proves nothing. Repair the fixture, not the arm."
              aborted = true
              break
            end
            deleted << rel
            next
          end

          rel, needle, replacement = op
          body  = bodies[rel] || ST_FIXTURE.fetch(rel)
          after = body.sub(needle) { replacement }
          if after == body
            st_bad << "#{plant[:id]}: the fixture no longer contains #{needle.inspect} in #{rel}, so " \
                      "this arm mutates nothing and proves nothing. Repair the fixture, not the arm."
            aborted = true
            break
          end
          bodies[rel] = after
        end
        next if aborted

        if base_out.include?(plant[:expect])
          st_bad << "#{plant[:id]}: its marker already appears in the CLEAN baseline output, so the arm " \
                    "cannot tell a plant from a green run."
          next
        end

        begin
          bodies.each { |rel, body| File.write(File.join(tmp, rel), body) }
          # A `:delete` must leave the INDEX, not just the disk. Rule A's subject
          # is `git ls-files`, so a file removed from the working tree alone is
          # still tracked and the arm would prove nothing while reading green —
          # which is exactly what it did before this line existed.
          deleted.each do |rel|
            File.delete(File.join(tmp, rel))
            Open3.capture2e("git", "-C", tmp, *git_cfg, "rm", "-q", "--cached", "--", rel)
          end
          out, code = st_run.call
          if code.zero?
            st_bad << "#{plant[:id]}: planted #{plant[:what]} and the guard stayed GREEN."
          elsif !out.include?(plant[:expect])
            st_bad << "#{plant[:id]}: went red, but NOT under its own name — the output does not " \
                      "contain #{plant[:expect].inspect}, so a reader is sent to the wrong rule.\n" +
                      out.lines.grep(/✗/).first(3).map { |l| "      #{l.rstrip[0, 180]}" }.join("\n")
          else
            puts format("  %-6s %-34s %s", plant[:kind] == :vacuity ? "V-RED" : "RED", plant[:id],
                        plant[:what])
          end
        ensure
          bodies.each_key { |rel| File.write(File.join(tmp, rel), ST_FIXTURE.fetch(rel)) }
          deleted.each do |rel|
            File.write(File.join(tmp, rel), ST_FIXTURE.fetch(rel))
            Open3.capture2e("git", "-C", tmp, *git_cfg, "add", "--", rel)
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end

  if st_bad.empty?
    kinds = ST_PLANTS.group_by { |p| p[:kind] }.transform_values(&:size)
    puts "check-gem-packaging --self-test: OK — #{ST_PLANTS.length} arm(s) " \
         "(#{kinds.fetch(:break, 0)} planted breaks, #{kinds.fetch(:vacuity, 0)} vacuity) over " \
         "#{ST_PLANTS.map { |p| p[:rule] }.uniq.length} rules, every one red under its own name."
    exit 0
  end

  warn "\ncheck-gem-packaging --self-test FAILED:\n"
  st_bad.each { |b| warn "  ✗ #{b}" }
  warn "\nA rule that no longer reddens on its own break is a rule that has stopped running, and the " \
       "bare exit code cannot tell you which one it is."
  exit 1
end

# ── Manifest A: what may legitimately stay out of a gem ──────────────────────
#
# Keys are fnmatch patterns against the path RELATIVE to the gem directory.
# Values are the reason that kind of file is development scaffolding rather
# than something a consumer installs. Anything not matched here must ship.
NOT_PACKAGED = {
  ".gitignore" => "git metadata. Nothing loads it, and RubyGems never consults it.",

  ".rspec" => "RSpec CLI defaults for running THIS gem's own suite in this " \
              "checkout. Consumers do not run it.",

  "Gemfile" => "the DEVELOPMENT bundle for this gem's own suite (path: entries " \
               "pointing at sibling gems in this monorepo, dev-only gems). A " \
               "consumer resolves against the gemspec's declared dependencies; " \
               "shipping this file would resolve nothing and mislead everything.",

  "Gemfile.lock" => "the lock for that development bundle. A LIBRARY must not " \
                    "ship a lock — the consumer's resolver owns version choice.",

  "Rakefile" => "the dev task entry point (rake spec, rake parity). Its tasks " \
                "need spec/ and the development bundle, neither of which ships, " \
                "so it would be a broken file in an installed gem.",

  "*.gemspec" => "RubyGems stores the built specification inside the .gem's own " \
                 "metadata. The source gemspec is not needed, and would be " \
                 "actively wrong to ship: its Dir.glob calls would re-run " \
                 "against the installed tree.",

  "spec/**/*" => "the gem's own RSpec suite, plus its fixtures and stubs. " \
                 "Consumers do not run it; shipping it puts spec_helper and " \
                 "test doubles on their load path.",

  "test/**/*" => "same as spec/**/*, for kiosk-rls-minitest, which uses the " \
                 "Minitest layout.",
}.freeze

# ── Manifest B: declared reaches outside the package ─────────────────────────
#
# gem => { literal path as written in the source => reason }. An entry here says
# "yes, this gem resolves a path that is not in its own package, and here is why
# that is known and accepted". Same shape, and the same intent, as `ungated:` in
# ci.yml's demos matrix and `except:` in bin/check-demo-copies: the divergence is
# recorded rather than invisible.
OUT_OF_GEM = {
  # Empty today. The one entry this manifest was born with — kiosk-redteam
  # shelling out to ../../../../kiosk-pow-equihash/solve.py — was closed the
  # way its reason foretold: kiosk-redteam now declares kiosk-pow-equihash as
  # a runtime dependency and asks Kiosk::Pow::Equihash.solver_path for the
  # solver's installed location. The mechanism stays for the next escape.
}.freeze

# Shapes of `__dir__`-relative path resolution this checker understands. Every
# other occurrence of __dir__/__FILE__ in packaged Ruby is a hard error — see
# THE FAIL-OPEN GUARD in the header.
RESOLVABLE = [
  /File\.expand_path\(\s*"([^"#]*)"\s*,\s*__dir__\s*\)/,
  /File\.expand_path\(\s*"([^"#]*)"\s*,\s*File\.dirname\(__FILE__\)\s*\)/,
  /File\.join\(\s*__dir__\s*,\s*"([^"#]*)"\s*\)/,
].freeze

# Packaged files Rule B reads. Everything here executes as Ruby inside the
# INSTALLED gem, so a __dir__ in one resolves against the package. `.tt` is
# excluded on purpose — see THE FAIL-OPEN GUARD in the header.
RUBY_BEARING = /\.(rb|erb|rake|ru|gemspec)\z/.freeze

# ── Rule C: the install pointer (K-1343) ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# THE POINTER IS DERIVED, NEVER TYPED. Both halves come out of the monorepo
# README: the repository slug off the CANONICAL install line it declares, and
# the anchor off its own Install heading. So the day the gems are published and
# that line loses its `github:` qualifier, this rule reports that it can no
# longer compute its subject and FAILS — which is the moment a human restates
# it, not a moment to pass quietly. (Same discipline, and the same reason, as
# the staleness checks on NOT_PACKAGED and OUT_OF_GEM below.)
MONOREPO_README    = "README.md"
CANONICAL_GEM_LINE = /^gem\s+"kiosk-all"\s*,\s*github:\s*"([A-Za-z0-9._\/-]+)"/
INSTALL_HEADING    = /^##\s+Install\s*$/

# What counts as TEACHING an install. Three shapes, because there are three
# ways to say it and a reader follows any of them: the Gemfile line, and the
# two commands. Deliberately not anchored to a fenced block — the shape that
# started this was a `#` comment in a packaged `.rb`, with no fence in sight.
INSTALL_TEACHING = [
  /^\s*gem\s+"kiosk-[a-z0-9-]+"/,
  /bundle add\s+kiosk[a-z0-9-]*/,
  /gem install\s+kiosk[a-z0-9-]*/,
].freeze

# Packaged files the pointer rule reads. A file it cannot decode as UTF-8 is an
# ERROR rather than a skip, for the reason THE FAIL-OPEN GUARD gives for Rule B:
# "the regex found nothing because nobody opened the file" is the failure this
# script exists to make impossible.
TEXT_BEARING = /\.(md|rb|erb|rake|ru|gemspec|txt|py|yml|yaml|tt)\z/.freeze

# gem => { packaged path => reason } for a file that MENTIONS an install
# command without instructing anyone to run it. An entry here says "this
# occurrence is prose ABOUT the resolver, not a snippet a reader will copy".
# Checked for staleness like every other manifest in this file: an entry whose
# file no longer matches any INSTALL_TEACHING shape, or is no longer packaged,
# is a recorded exception for a problem that has gone away.
INSTALL_MENTION_ONLY = {
  "kiosk-server" => {
    "lib/kiosk/server/request_validation.rb" =>
      "the ConfigurationError rationale explains what a MISSING runtime dependency means by " \
      "naming the resolution `gem install kiosk-server` performs. It is a statement about the " \
      "gemspec, mid-sentence, and instructs the reader to run `bundle install` — never to " \
      "install this gem from a registry that does not have it.",
  },
}.freeze

errors = []
used_patterns = {}
used_out_of_gem = {}
used_mention_only = {}
listing = []
packaged_index = {}
pointer_subjects = 0

# ── Rule C's derivation, once, before any gem is built ───────────────────────
readme_src   = File.read(File.join(ROOT, MONOREPO_README))
slug         = readme_src[CANONICAL_GEM_LINE, 1]
has_heading  = readme_src.match?(INSTALL_HEADING)
install_ref  = (slug && has_heading) ? "https://github.com/#{slug}#install" : nil

if slug.nil?
  errors << "#{MONOREPO_README} declares no canonical `gem \"kiosk-all\", github: \"...\"` line, so " \
            "Rule C cannot derive where an install snippet should point. If the gems have been " \
            "PUBLISHED and the qualifier is gone, that is the moment to restate this rule in " \
            "bin/check-gem-packaging — not the moment for it to match nothing and pass."
end
if !has_heading
  errors << "#{MONOREPO_README} has no `## Install` heading, so Rule C cannot derive the anchor " \
            "an install snippet should point at. Restore the heading or re-derive the anchor in " \
            "bin/check-gem-packaging."
end

gemspecs = Dir[File.join(ROOT, "*", "*.gemspec")].sort
abort "check-gem-packaging: no */*.gemspec under #{ROOT}" if gemspecs.empty?

Dir.mktmpdir do |tmp|
  gemspecs.each do |gemspec|
    dir  = File.dirname(gemspec)
    gem  = File.basename(dir)
    out  = File.join(tmp, "#{gem}.gem")
    log  = File.join(tmp, "#{gem}.log")

    # `gem build` does NOT chdir into the gemspec's directory — every gemspec
    # here calls Dir.glob, which resolves against CWD, so building from the repo
    # root produces a spec whose files do not exist. Build from the gem dir,
    # exactly as a release would.
    unless system("cd #{dir.shellescape} && gem build #{File.basename(gemspec).shellescape} " \
                  "-o #{out.shellescape} >#{log.shellescape} 2>&1")
      errors << "#{gem}: `gem build` FAILED — the gem cannot be packaged at all:\n" \
                "#{File.read(log).lines.map { |l| "      #{l}" }.join}"
      next
    end

    # Read the file list back OUT of the built package rather than off the
    # gemspec: `spec.files` is a promise, the tar is the artifact. (RubyGems
    # silently drops directory entries at build time, so the two already differ.)
    package  = Gem::Package.new(out)
    packaged = package.contents.sort
    unpacked = File.join(tmp, "#{gem}-unpacked")
    package.extract_files(unpacked)

    tracked  = `cd #{ROOT.shellescape} && git ls-files -- #{gem.shellescape}`
               .lines.map { |l| l.chomp.delete_prefix("#{gem}/") }
    if tracked.empty?
      errors << "#{gem}: `git ls-files` returned nothing — is this a checkout?"
      next
    end

    # ── Rule A: completeness ──────────────────────────────────────────────
    (tracked - packaged).each do |rel|
      pattern = NOT_PACKAGED.keys.find do |pat|
        File.fnmatch?(pat, rel, File::FNM_PATHNAME | File::FNM_DOTMATCH)
      end
      if pattern
        (used_patterns[pattern] ||= []) << gem
        next
      end
      errors << "#{gem}: `#{rel}` is tracked in this repo but is NOT in the built gem. " \
                "Someone installing #{gem} from RubyGems does not get it. Either add it to " \
                "`spec.files` in #{gem}.gemspec, or — if it is development scaffolding — add " \
                "a pattern for it to NOT_PACKAGED in bin/check-gem-packaging with the reason."
    end

    # ── Rule B: reachability ──────────────────────────────────────────────
    reaches = []
    packaged.grep(RUBY_BEARING).each do |rel|
      src  = File.read(File.join(unpacked, rel))
      here = File.dirname(rel)

      literals = RESOLVABLE.flat_map { |re| src.scan(re).map(&:first) }

      literals.each do |literal|
        # Resolve against a sentinel root so escaping the gem is detectable.
        abs    = File.expand_path(literal, File.join("/GEM", here))
        inside = abs.start_with?("/GEM/") || abs == "/GEM"
        target = inside ? abs.delete_prefix("/GEM/") : literal
        reaches << [rel, literal, target, inside]

        if (reason = OUT_OF_GEM.dig(gem, literal))
          errors << "#{gem}: OUT_OF_GEM entry for `#{literal}` carries no reason" if reason.strip.empty?
          (used_out_of_gem[gem] ||= []) << literal
          next
        end

        unless inside
          errors << "#{gem}: `#{rel}` resolves `#{literal}` against __dir__, which escapes the " \
                    "gem root entirely — it can only exist in this monorepo checkout, never in an " \
                    "installed gem. Depend on whatever owns that file and ask it for the path, or " \
                    "declare it in OUT_OF_GEM in bin/check-gem-packaging with the reason."
          next
        end

        ok = packaged.include?(target) ||
             packaged.any? { |f| f.start_with?("#{target}/") }
        next if ok

        errors << "#{gem}: `#{rel}` resolves `#{literal}` against __dir__ to `#{target}`, which is " \
                  "NOT in the built gem. It works here only because every consumer in this repo " \
                  "uses `path:` and reads the working tree; from RubyGems this file is missing at " \
                  "runtime. Add it to `spec.files` in #{gem}.gemspec."
      end

      # Fail-open guard: every __dir__/__FILE__ occurrence must be one we read.
      seen = src.scan(/__dir__|__FILE__/).size
      next if seen <= literals.size

      errors << "#{gem}: `#{rel}` contains #{seen} __dir__/__FILE__ occurrence(s) but " \
                "bin/check-gem-packaging could only resolve #{literals.size}. It does not " \
                "understand the shape used here, so it cannot tell whether the path it builds is " \
                "packaged — and an unreadable shape must not pass silently. Write the path as " \
                "`File.expand_path(\"literal\", __dir__)`, or teach RESOLVABLE the new shape."
    end

    # ── Rule C: the install pointer ───────────────────────────────────────
    packaged.grep(TEXT_BEARING).each do |rel|
      src = File.read(File.join(unpacked, rel), encoding: "UTF-8")
      unless src.valid_encoding?
        errors << "#{gem}: `#{rel}` has a #{TEXT_BEARING.source} extension but is not valid UTF-8, " \
                  "so Rule C could not read it and cannot say whether it teaches an install. An " \
                  "unreadable file must not pass silently — give it an extension outside " \
                  "TEXT_BEARING in bin/check-gem-packaging, with the reason."
        next
      end
      next unless INSTALL_TEACHING.any? { |re| src.match?(re) }

      pointer_subjects += 1
      if (reason = INSTALL_MENTION_ONLY.dig(gem, rel))
        errors << "#{gem}: INSTALL_MENTION_ONLY entry for `#{rel}` carries no reason" if reason.strip.empty?
        (used_mention_only[gem] ||= []) << rel
        next
      end
      next if install_ref && src.include?(install_ref)
      next if install_ref.nil? # already reported once, above; do not repeat it per file

      errors << "#{gem}: `#{rel}` is IN THE BUILT GEM and teaches an install of a Kiosk gem, but " \
                "does not point at #{install_ref}. No Kiosk gem is on RubyGems, so a reader who " \
                "has only this package follows an instruction that answers 404 and has nowhere to " \
                "find out why. Add the pointer to that section — it is where publication status " \
                "is stated canonically — or, if this occurrence is prose ABOUT the resolver rather " \
                "than a snippet anyone will copy, declare it in INSTALL_MENTION_ONLY in " \
                "bin/check-gem-packaging with the reason."
    end

    packaged_index[gem] = packaged
    listing << [gem, packaged.size, tracked.size, reaches]
  end
end

# A pattern that guards nothing is a pattern nobody will re-read.
(NOT_PACKAGED.keys - used_patterns.keys).each do |pat|
  errors << "NOT_PACKAGED pattern `#{pat}` matched no tracked-but-unpackaged file in any gem — " \
            "it is stale. Drop it from bin/check-gem-packaging."
end
NOT_PACKAGED.each do |pat, reason|
  errors << "NOT_PACKAGED pattern `#{pat}` carries no reason" if reason.to_s.strip.empty?
end
OUT_OF_GEM.each do |gem, literals|
  unless listing.any? { |(g, *)| g == gem }
    errors << "OUT_OF_GEM names #{gem}, which has no gemspec in this repo — stale entry."
    next
  end

  # The same argument as the NOT_PACKAGED staleness check above, and it bites
  # harder here: this manifest's entries exist to be DELETED once the escape is
  # fixed or decided, so an entry whose literal nobody reaches for any more is a
  # recorded exception for a problem that no longer exists.
  (literals.keys - (used_out_of_gem[gem] || [])).each do |literal|
    errors << "OUT_OF_GEM declares `#{literal}` for #{gem}, but no packaged file in #{gem} " \
              "resolves that literal against __dir__ any more. Either the escape was fixed — " \
              "delete the entry — or the code moved to a shape bin/check-gem-packaging cannot " \
              "read, which is worse than the escape."
  end
end

# Rule C's own staleness and vacuity, in that order.
INSTALL_MENTION_ONLY.each do |gem, files|
  unless listing.any? { |(g, *)| g == gem }
    errors << "INSTALL_MENTION_ONLY names #{gem}, which has no gemspec in this repo — stale entry."
    next
  end

  (files.keys - (used_mention_only[gem] || [])).each do |rel|
    errors << "INSTALL_MENTION_ONLY declares `#{rel}` for #{gem}, but that file is no longer " \
              "packaged, or no longer mentions installing a Kiosk gem at all. Either the sentence " \
              "was rewritten — delete the entry — or the mention moved to a shape " \
              "INSTALL_TEACHING cannot see, which is the exemption outliving the thing it excused."
  end
end

# VACUITY, and note the polarity: Rule C is a PRESENCE requirement, so «the
# detector matched nothing» is not the rule being obeyed — it is the rule not
# running. Eleven gem READMEs teach an install today; a run that finds zero has
# lost its subject, not gained a clean tree.
if install_ref && pointer_subjects.zero?
  errors << "Rule C examined every packaged file in #{listing.size} gems and found NONE that " \
            "teaches an install of a Kiosk gem. That is not a pass: this rule is a presence " \
            "requirement, so an empty subject set means INSTALL_TEACHING has stopped matching " \
            "the shapes people write, and the rule is holding nothing. Fix the patterns in " \
            "bin/check-gem-packaging, or — if no packaged file really teaches an install any " \
            "more — retire the rule deliberately."
end

if ARGV.include?("--list")
  puts format("%-22s %5s %5s  %-46s %s", "GEM", "PKG", "REPO", "REACHED FROM __dir__", "IN THE GEM?")
  listing.each do |(gem, npkg, ntracked, reaches)|
    rows = reaches.uniq { |(_rel, _lit, target, _inside)| target }
    puts format("%-22s %5d %5d  %-46s %s", gem, npkg, ntracked,
                rows.empty? ? "—" : "", rows.empty? ? "—" : "")
    rows.each do |(_rel, literal, target, inside)|
      status =
        if !inside                                                            then "no — ESCAPES the gem root (declared in OUT_OF_GEM)"
        elsif packaged_index[gem].include?(target)                            then "yes (file)"
        elsif packaged_index[gem].any? { |f| f.start_with?("#{target}/") }    then "yes (directory, #{packaged_index[gem].count { |f| f.start_with?("#{target}/") }} files)"
        else                                                                       "NO"
        end
      puts format("%-22s %5s %5s  %-46s %s", "", "", "", inside ? target : literal, status)
    end
  end
  puts "\nPKG = files in the built .gem, REPO = files git tracks under the gem directory; the " \
       "difference is declared in NOT_PACKAGED."
  # Deliberately falls through to the verdict below rather than exiting here:
  # `--list` that returned 1 without printing WHY would be a check that fails
  # silently, which is the one thing this file must never do.
end

if errors.empty?
  puts "check-gem-packaging: OK — #{listing.size} gems build; every tracked file is packaged or " \
       "declared, and every __dir__-relative path resolves inside the package; " \
       "#{pointer_subjects} packaged file(s) teach an install and each points at #{install_ref} " \
       "(derived from #{MONOREPO_README}) or is declared prose-only"
  exit 0
end

warn "\ncheck-gem-packaging FAILED:\n"
errors.each { |e| warn "  ✗ #{e}" }
warn "\nA gem is what the .gem file contains, not what the working tree contains. Every consumer " \
     "in this repo uses `path:`, so nothing here can tell the two apart — this check is the only " \
     "thing standing between a missing file and someone's `gem install`."
exit 1
